<article class="pv-article">
	<p class="pv-eyebrow">About</p>
	<h1>Gavin Keulks</h1>

	<p class="pv-lead">
		I am a professor of literature and film at Western Oregon University, where
		I have taught since 1999 and directed the Honors Program from 2008 to 2027.
		My work centres on twentieth-century English and Irish fiction of the
		postwar period, and extends into film studies, literary history, and
		critical theory.
	</p>

	<p>
		I have published three books. <em>Father and Son: Kingsley Amis, Martin
		Amis, and the British Novel Since 1950</em> (University of Wisconsin Press)
		is still the only extended study of that literary inheritance.{' '}
		<em>Martin Amis: Postmodernism and Beyond</em> (Palgrave Macmillan) was the
		first collection of original essays on Amis. <em>The Cambridge Companion to
		Martin Amis</em> (Cambridge University Press, 2026) brings together fourteen
		scholars from five countries and stands as the definitive account of his
		fifty-year career.
	</p>

	<blockquote class="pv-quote">
		<p>
			Holding and absorbing, filled with excellently shrewd and steady
			analysis.
		</p>
		<cite>Dale Salwak, on <em>Father and Son</em></cite>
	</blockquote>

	<p>
		Since 2006 I have built and edited <a href="https://martinamisweb.com">the
		Martin Amis Web</a>, the authoritative digital archive for the author. The
		British Library named it a site of scholarly significance in 2012 and has
		archived it twice a year since, as part of the UK Web Archive.
	</p>

	<p>
		Two projects are now underway. The first is <em>Flight</em>, a novel &mdash;
		an eighty-thousand-word manuscript I am preparing for publication. The
		second is a scholarly book on the reclamation of traumatic spaces in
		Ireland, built from fieldwork and my own photographs.
	</p>

